\chapter{Screening Method}
For each bearing housing we compute the band-limited RMS velocity $x_{\mathrm{rms}}$ between 10 Hz and 1 kHz, together with the crest factor $c_{\mathrm{peak}}$ of the same band. Both quantities are available from the handheld meter directly, so the method requires no additional instrumentation on the route.

The two quantities are combined into a single screening score
\begin{equation}
s = 0.35 \, x_{\mathrm{rms}} + 0.65 \, c_{\mathrm{peak}},
\end{equation}
where the weights were chosen so that a healthy housing in our reference fleet scores below 2.0 and the historically failed units scored above 3.0 in the six months preceding failure. Housings scoring between 2.0 and 3.0 are re-measured on the next shift rather than triggering an immediate work order.

The score is deliberately simple: it must be computable with the two numbers the meter already prints, it must be explainable to the crews in one sentence, and it must fail safe, meaning that a misread gauge or a skipped housing biases the score upward into the re-measure band rather than downward into silence.

\chapter{Calibration Analysis}
Pooling fourteen months of paired logs from the two depots confirms what the crews suspected: the handheld meters read systematically high relative to the installed reference sensors, and the offset grows with the measured value rather than staying constant. A plain additive correction is therefore inadequate.

A least-squares fit on the 1{,}904 paired readings gives the working calibration
\begin{equation}
T_{\mathrm{adj}} = 0.63 \, T_{\mathrm{raw}} + 1.2,
\end{equation}
where $T_{\mathrm{raw}}$ is the handheld reading in mm/s and $T_{\mathrm{adj}}$ is the value entered into the screening worksheet. The fit residuals show no remaining trend across the operating range, and a holdout quarter of the pairs reproduces the fitted coefficients to two decimal places.

Applying the calibration retrospectively to the paper logs resolves most of the apparent disagreements between crews that were reported in last year's review. The remaining discrepancies cluster on housings adjacent to the main drive motor, where structure-borne vibration contaminates the handheld reading regardless of scaling, and we recommend relocating those measurement points rather than complicating the calibration.

We considered fitting separate calibrations per depot but rejected the idea: the two fits differ by less than their standard errors, and a single shared formula keeps the route card identical at both sites, which matters for the crews who rotate between depots.
